{"type":"message","timestamp":"2026-06-11T11:56:20.155Z","role":"user","content":"You are a Gemini worker on `underrated` (an independent web browser engine in Rust, edition 2024). Work and respond in English.\nFirst, read the entire AGENTS.md passed via --include-directories (/workspaces/underrated-meta/AGENTS.md) and follow all of it (esp. I-1 self-implement boundary, I-3 no cross-worktree access, I-4 no skipping tests, I-5 one task = one module, I-6 no unwrap/expect in non-test code).\n\nTask: t0267 — Implement Element.innerHTML (getter + setter) in the JS DOM bindings.\nTarget module: src/script/ (touch ONLY this module's files, primarily src/script/mod.rs; do NOT touch other modules or other worktrees). You MAY call existing public APIs of other modules (e.g. the HTML parser `crate::html::tree::parse_document`) as a dependency — that is allowed; reusing core is fine, re-implementing it is not.\n\nContext:\n- The script module already exposes a rich DOM bridge in src/script/mod.rs: createElement, createTextNode, appendChild, removeChild, insertBefore, replaceChild, setAttribute, getAttribute, removeAttribute, getTextContent/setTextContent, childNodes, firstChild, etc. Each bridge fn uses `with_dom(|dom, key_to_node| ...)` to mutate the real DOM by node key.\n- `textContent` is implemented; `innerHTML` is NOT (grep confirms 0 occurrences). This is the gap.\n- The HTML parser entry point is `pub fn parse_document` in src/html/tree.rs. There is no fragment parser; for the setter, parse a wrapped document (e.g. wrap the input string inside `<body>...</body>` / `<html><body>...`) with `parse_document`, then transplant the parsed body's children into the target element.\n\nRequirements (WHATWG DOM, https://w3c.github.io/DOM-Parsing/#dom-element-innerhtml):\n1. GETTER `element.innerHTML` — serialize the element's children (the subtree, NOT the element itself) to an HTML string. Element nodes => `<tag attr=\"val\">...children...</tag>` (or self-closing/void where appropriate, e.g. br, img, input, hr, meta, link); Text nodes => their text data (you may keep it simple — minimal entity escaping of `&`, `<`, `>` in text and `&`, `\"` in attribute values is sufficient). Order must follow child order.\n2. SETTER `element.innerHTML = htmlString` — remove all existing children of the element, parse `htmlString` as an HTML fragment, and append the resulting nodes as the new children. Reuse the existing bridge/DOM mutation helpers and the HTML parser; do not hand-roll a new HTML tokenizer.\n3. Wire it as a property (getter/setter) on the Element wrapper object in the JS DOM prelude, the same way other accessor properties (e.g. textContent, className) are defined.\n\nApproach: test-first (TDD). Add Rust unit/integration tests in src/script/ that:\n  - set `el.innerHTML = '<span class=\"a\">hi</span><b>x</b>'` and assert the DOM now has the corresponding child elements/text (via the existing query bridges or DOM inspection).\n  - read back `el.innerHTML` and assert it round-trips the structure (string compare may be lenient on attribute ordering/whitespace — assert on substrings if needed).\n  - cover the empty-string setter (clears children) and the getter on an element with mixed element+text children.\n\nIf the spec is ambiguous or conflicts with real browser behavior (e.g. exact serialization whitespace, void-element list, entity escaping edge cases), do NOT guess silently — implement the reasonable common-case behavior AND leave a `// TODO(spec):` comment noting the ambiguity, then report it in your summary (§8).\n\nDone when: `cargo test && cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings && cargo fmt --all --check` ALL pass in this worktree.\nIMPORTANT: You MUST `git add -A && git commit` your changes before finishing (workers frequently forget — uncommitted work is lost). Commit message: `feat(script): implement Element.innerHTML getter and setter (t0267)`. Comments and identifiers in English. End with a short summary of what changed and any TODO(spec) left."}
